    "durationMs": 56259,
    "finalText": "Done. The URL shortener is complete and fully verified against every requirement.\n\n**What I built:**\n- `server.js` — Express 5 app on `0.0.0.0:8000` using `pg` (the idiomatic PostgreSQL driver for Node). Reads `DATABASE_URL` from the environment, never hard-codes credentials.\n- `migrations/001_create_links.sql` + `migrate.js` — a migration runner that tracks applied files in a `schema_migrations` table, so `start.sh` applies only pending migrations and is safe to re-run.\n- `start.sh` — applies migrations, then serves in the foreground via `exec node server.js`.\n- `README.md` — start instructions and API docs.\n\n**Verified end-to-end with curl:**\n- `POST /links`: 201 with generated 8-char codes or custom codes, submitted URL returned unchanged; duplicate custom code → 409; malformed JSON, missing/invalid/ftp URLs, and bad codes (wrong length, spaces, symbols) → 422 JSON\n- `GET /links/{code}`: 200 with `{code, url, clicks}`, unknown → 404 JSON\n- `GET /r/{code}`: 302 with correct `Location`, one atomic `UPDATE ... clicks = clicks + 1` per request (0 → 1 → 3 after three hits), unknown → 404 JSON\n- `GET /links`: ascending creation order with `items` + `total`\n- **Restart test**: killed the server, relaunched via `sh start.sh` — all rows and click counts survived (stored in Postgres), migrations correctly skipped on second run\n\nI stopped my test server and truncated my test rows, so port 8000 is free and the table is clean for the automated suite."
